\begin{abstract}
Cloud-native microservices routinely rely on timeouts and retries to mask transient failures. However, poorly tuned policies can amplify load, trigger retry storms, and push systems into metastable failure regimes. This report studies timeouts, retries, and load-limiting mechanisms---both individually and in combination---through a queueing-theoretic lens and with a microservice simulator that models services as $M/G/m/C$ queues with fault injection and configurable resilience policies. We formalize \emph{amplification budgets} that bound the retry-induced increase in offered load and discuss practical controls: circuit breakers, bulkheads, concurrency/rate limiters, retry budgets, and adaptive timeouts. We also outline how these controls interact along call chains, where independent per-hop retries can multiplicatively overload downstream tiers. The \emph{Simulator Experiments} and \emph{Cluster Experiments} sections are intentionally left blank for now; they will report emergent behaviors and validate the model on a Docker/Kubernetes deployment in subsequent iterations. Our goal is to motivate a dynamic, adaptive retry framework that closes the loop using online telemetry.
\end{abstract}

