\section{Related Work}
Bronson et al. analyze \emph{metastable failures} in distributed systems, showing how load amplification sustains failure even after the initial trigger disappears~\cite{bronsonMetastableFailuresDistributed2021}. This motivates explicit amplification budgets and fail-fast controls.

Service-mesh circuit breaking has been studied both in initial designs and subsequent evolutions, with guidance on configuring breakers from service topologies and on interactions with retry policies~\cite{sedghpourServiceMeshCircuit2021,salehsedghpourBreakingViciousCircle2023}. These works inform breaker signals and thresholds and emphasize the need to coordinate retries with protection mechanisms.

Aderaldo et al. measure the impact of retry patterns on latency and availability for single microservices, providing empirical baselines for benign versus harmful regimes~\cite{aderaldoHowRetryPattern2023}. Hanada et al. conduct an empirical study of request retries, highlighting pitfalls when timeouts and retries are naïvely combined~\cite{hanadaEmpiricalStudyRequest2024}. Together, these studies reinforce the need for retry budgets, per-error policies, and adaptive control.

\section{Conclusion}
Timeouts and retries are essential for masking transient failures, but without guardrails they can create self-inflicted overload. Modeling services as open $M/G/m/C$ queues clarifies stability conditions and yields a practical \emph{amplification budget} that bounds safe retrying. A policy stack of timeouts, circuit breakers, concurrency/rate limits, and retry budgets keeps systems on the safe side of $\rho<1$ even under partial failures and call-chain multiplication. The forthcoming simulated and cluster experiments will quantify these trade-offs and evaluate a simple adaptive controller that respects budgets and SLOs.

